# TODO - Native Linux packaging

## Objective

Produce portable Linux x86-64 executables with local asset discovery.

## Acceptance

- Do not link or bundle original game executables or libraries.
- Verify startup, save paths, packaging and Linux gameplay smoke tests.

## Evidence

The selected-title release build produced an 835,768-byte dynamically
linked x86-64 ELF executable on the development host. Its direct dynamic
dependencies were SDL2, libgcc and libc; it does not use the original game
executable or library. The hidden native four-player movement smoke passed
with four verified local art bundles. This verifies local startup, not
portable distribution, independent asset discovery, saves or complete
gameplay. Those remain open.

The native `package TITLE OUTPUT` command now compiles the admitted
title, preflights bounded executable and licence sources, and writes
them into a new private directory. It rejects an existing destination,
unknown title, wrong ELF target and unreviewed dependency licence
metadata. Original media, imported canonical assets and system
libraries are not bundled. An I/O failure can leave a partial directory;
the receipt is written only after the other files.

The first local package contained a 984,632-byte x86-64 ELF and 59
non-executable files. Its recorded SHA-256 matched independent hashing.
Reinvocation at the same destination was rejected. From the package
directory, the hidden touch, pose, enemy and dummy-audio smoke passed
using explicitly selected external bundles. The package remains local
to the development host. Linux ABI portability, automatic local asset
discovery, save paths and complete gameplay remain open.
